(() => {
 'use strict';
 const esc=v=>String(v??'').replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
 const localCaptionText={"parallax": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.860\nAnnual parallax uses Earth's orbit as a change in\n\n00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:06.040\nviewpoint. Six months apart, Earth is on opposite sides\n\n00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.840\nof the Sun. A nearby star appears to shift\n\n00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.520\nagainst more distant stars. The star does not jump\n\n00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.160\nsideways in space. Move the same star farther away,\n\n00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.560\nand its angular shift becomes smaller. The parallax angle\n\n00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.050\nis half the total six-month displacement. With parallax in\n\n00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:26.520\narcseconds, distance in parsecs equals one divided by parallax.\n\n00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.380\nA parallax of one tenth of an arcsecond means\n\n00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:33.312\nten parsecs. Twice the distance gives half the parallax.\n", "brightness": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480\nLuminosity is the energy a star emits each second.\n\n00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.400\nApparent brightness is the energy arriving each second per\n\n00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.220\nsquare meter. Watch the same outgoing light spread across\n\n00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:15.000\nlarger spheres. Double the distance while keeping luminosity fixed.\n\n00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.360\nThe sphere has four times the area, so a\n\n00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.540\ndetector receives one quarter as much light. Triple the\n\n00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:24.520\ndistance gives one ninth. Equal apparent brightness does not\n\n00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.460\nmean equal luminosity. A star twice as far away\n\n00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:31.360\nneeds four times the luminosity to look equally bright.\n\n00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.488\nThese comparisons assume no intervening extinction.\n", "magnitude": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.740\nOn the magnitude scale, a smaller number means a\n\n00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:06.280\nbrighter object. A difference of five magnitudes corresponds to\n\n00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:09.600\na brightness factor of one hundred. Watch brightness fall\n\n00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.640\nas the magnitude number rises. Absolute magnitude means apparent\n\n00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:17.720\nmagnitude at ten parsecs, with extinction removed. Moving the\n\n00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:20.380\nsame star from ten to one hundred parsecs makes\n\n00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.400\nit one hundred times fainter and increases its apparent\n\n00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.050\nmagnitude by five. Dust also makes a star fainter.\n\n00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.780\nIf you attribute all that dimming to distance, you\n\n00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:35.820\noverestimate the distance. Keep intrinsic luminosity, distance, and extinction\n\n00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.808\nseparate when interpreting apparent magnitude.\n", "color": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.580\nAs surface temperature rises, the peak of a thermal\n\n00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:06.680\nspectrum shifts toward shorter wavelengths. A hotter star emits\n\n00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:10.380\nmore blue light relative to visible light. Watch the\n\n00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.720\nspectral curve change, not just its label. The lecture\n\n00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.384\ncompares the visual to blue brightness ratio: about one\n\n00:00:17.384 --> 00:00:19.824\npoint eight seven for the Sun and zero point\n\n00:00:19.824 --> 00:00:23.140\neight one for Bellatrix. The hotter star has the\n\n00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:26.560\nsmaller visual to blue ratio. Now keep the star\n\n00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.560\nhot but put dust in the line of sight.\n\n00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.460\nDust preferentially removes blue light from the direct beam.\n\n00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.460\nA red observed color can mean a cooler star\n\n00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.680\nor a reddened hotter star; color alone does not\n\n00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.816\nresolve that ambiguity.\n", "binary": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120\nBinary stars orbit their common center of mass. When\n\n00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.060\na star approaches us, its spectral lines shift toward\n\n00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.100\nblue; when it recedes, they shift toward red. Watch\n\n00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.760\nthe orbital motion and line position change together. Orbital\n\n00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.200\nmotion provides mass evidence. It is different from measuring\n\n00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.280\na color or an apparent brightness. The example is\n\n00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:25.020\nschematic: a real mass determination needs sufficient orbital information,\n\n00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:29.100\nincluding the viewing geometry. Radius follows a different relation:\n\n00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.280\nluminosity is proportional to radius squared times surface temperature\n\n00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.320\nto the fourth power. At fixed temperature, doubling radius\n\n00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.260\ngives four times the luminosity. Keep the Stefan Boltzmann\n\n00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.704\nlaw separate from binary dynamics.\n", "hr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.700\nOn this Hertzsprung Russell diagram, luminosity increases upward and\n\n00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:07.760\nsurface temperature decreases toward the right. Moving to the\n\n00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.700\nright means cooler, not hotter. Use both coordinates to\n\n00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.500\ninterpret a star. At the same surface temperature, a\n\n00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.540\nmore luminous star has a larger radius. Watch two\n\n00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.340\nstars share a temperature while their emitting areas and\n\n00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.620\npositions differ. Giants can be cool and still very\n\n00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:26.960\nluminous. At the same luminosity, a hotter star must\n\n00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:30.960\nbe smaller. Hot but faint white dwarfs occupy the\n\n00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.140\nlower left. These comparisons show relationships, not the evolutionary\n\n00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.360\ntrack of one star; the main sequence is not\n\n00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:40.272\na timeline from left to right.\n", "nebula": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420\nIn an emission nebula, energetic ultraviolet light from hot\n\n00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:09.120\nstars ionizes gas. Recombination and subsequent transitions produce characteristic\n\n00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:13.140\nemission. The glowing gas supplies the visible light. A\n\n00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.980\nreflection nebula redirects light from nearby stars toward us.\n\n00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.460\nDust scattering makes these regions often appear blue. The\n\n00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.580\ncloud is reflecting starlight rather than generating the same\n\n00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:27.500\ngas emission spectrum. A dark nebula blocks background light\n\n00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:30.340\nalong our line of sight. The same dusty cloud\n\n00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:34.340\ncan look different with illumination and viewing geometry. Infrared\n\n00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.940\nsuffers less dust extinction than visible light, allowing observations\n\n00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.200\ndeeper inside; it does not pass through every cloud\n\n00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.728\ncompletely unhindered.\n", "formation": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.140\nBegin with a dense core inside a dark cloud.\n\n00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:07.080\nGravity brings material inward. Bok globules are dusty star\n\n00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.380\nformation sites; a dark appearance does not mean nothing\n\n00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.820\nis happening inside. The supplied sequence develops a central\n\n00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:18.080\nprotostar, an envelope, and a rotating disk. Bipolar outflows\n\n00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.920\nfollow the rotation axis. Herbig Haro features are associated\n\n00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.600\nwith outflow activity, not a different name for the\n\n00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.700\nentire dark cloud. The sequence continues through a young\n\n00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:30.240\nstar with a disk toward a stellar and planetary\n\n00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.360\nsystem. The envelope thins and the disk changes. This\n\n00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.840\nis a schematic path, not a guarantee that every\n\n00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.700\ncore forms one star or follows the same ages\n\n00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:41.088\nand sizes.\n", "collapse": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520\nCold, dense cloud regions are favorable for collapse: gravity\n\n00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:07.000\ncan overcome thermal pressure support. Dense does not automatically\n\n00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.980\nmean hot, and a bright ionized region is not\n\n00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.040\nthe same thing as a cold star forming core.\n\n00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.780\nContraction releases gravitational energy and heats the interior. Rising\n\n00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:22.540\ndensity and temperature raise gas pressure, slowing contraction. This\n\n00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:25.560\ndoes not require hydrogen fusion to have started, and\n\n00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.680\ninterior heating does not mean surface temperature always rises\n\n00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:33.360\nalong every track. The object keeps radiating energy. Without\n\n00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.460\nsustained replacement, cooling reduces pressure support and contraction resumes.\n\n00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.180\nApproximate force balance is different from a long term\n\n00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.800\nenergy balance. Luminosity may rise or fall because both\n\n00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.168\nradius and surface temperature matter.\n", "stellar-clocks": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160\nA passing shock can compress a cold cloud.\n\n00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.040\nDense fragments can then collapse under gravity. The\n\n00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.180\nshock is a trigger, not a guarantee that\n\n00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:09.500\nevery cloud becomes a star.\n\n00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:13.780\nAs a protostar contracts, gravitational energy becomes heat\n\n00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.180\nand radiation. The Kelvin Helmholtz time is proportional\n\n00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:21.440\nto mass squared, divided by radius times luminosity.\n\n00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.520\nHold mass and radius fixed: twice the luminosity\n\n00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.660\ngives half this characteristic time.\n\n00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.120\nOn the main sequence, hydrogen fusion supplies the\n\n00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.740\nenergy. The approximate lifetime instead scales as mass\n\n00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:37.780\ndivided by luminosity. Massive stars have more fuel,\n\n00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:41.080\nbut their much greater luminosity uses it faster.\n\n00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.240\nKeep these two stellar clocks separate.\n", "burning-pathways": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.820\nStart with the net result. The proton proton\n\n00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:06.440\nchain turns hydrogen into helium. The illustration combines\n\n00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.660\nfour hydrogen nuclei into one helium nucleus, with\n\n00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.860\nenergy and other products leaving. It summarizes the\n\n00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.520\nnet reaction rather than every intermediate step.\n\n00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.280\nThe C N O cycle also turns hydrogen\n\n00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:22.540\ninto helium. Carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen participate in\n\n00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:25.720\nintermediate steps. The catalyst cycle returns to its\n\n00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.580\nstarting nucleus while hydrogen has been consumed and\n\n00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:31.760\nhelium released. Hotter cores favor this route more\n\n00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:32.280\nstrongly.\n\n00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.660\nTriple alpha has a different fuel. Two helium\n\n00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:39.480\nnuclei briefly form beryllium; adding a third makes\n\n00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.800\ncarbon. Some carbon captures another helium nucleus to\n\n00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.280\nmake oxygen. In the lecture’s local approximations, triple\n\n00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.950\nalpha is more temperature sensitive than C N\n\n00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.280\nO, which is more sensitive than the proton\n\n00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.260\nproton chain.\n", "red-giant-evolution": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.140\nHydrogen becomes helium inside the core. At the\n\n00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:05.760\nsame mass density and temperature, this means fewer\n\n00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.840\npressure bearing particles. The core slowly contracts and\n\n00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.360\nheats. Hydrogen fusion speeds up, so the main\n\n00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.000\nsequence Sun gradually becomes more luminous.\n\n00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:17.400\nAfter core hydrogen is exhausted, the helium core\n\n00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.600\ncontracts while hydrogen burns around it in a\n\n00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.240\nshell. The envelope expands and its surface cools.\n\n00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.260\nThe hot core and cool outer surface are\n\n00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.460\ndifferent regions. This is a coupled structural response,\n\n00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:30.860\nnot a claim that every layer moves together.\n\n00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.540\nIn the supplied low mass model, helium ignites\n\n00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.460\nrapidly inside the core. This helium flash is\n\n00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.980\nnot a visible supernova. The core then expands\n\n00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.600\nsomewhat, the envelope shrinks and warms, and total\n\n00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.320\nluminosity falls. On the H R diagram, that\n\n00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:46.860\nchange moves down and left.\n", "cluster-clock": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160\nA cluster gives us stars with approximately the\n\n00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.200\nsame age and distance, but different masses. Higher\n\n00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.460\nmass members in this group are hotter and\n\n00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.200\nmore luminous. Common birth conditions make their different\n\n00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.760\nstages useful evidence for stellar evolution.\n\n00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.880\nThe luminous high mass members use their core\n\n00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.340\nhydrogen sooner. They leave the main sequence and\n\n00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:21.640\nchange structure. Their points move away from the\n\n00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:24.560\nupper main sequence as their envelopes expand and\n\n00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:27.260\ncool. These are changes in the stars, not\n\n00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.140\nstars moving across physical space.\n\n00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.800\nWith increasing age, the surviving main sequence ends\n\n00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.100\nat lower luminosity and cooler temperature. That bend\n\n00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.300\nis the turnoff. Use it rather than the\n\n00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.800\nbrightest red giant. An old globular cluster can\n\n00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:44.760\nstill contain hot helium burning stars, so a\n\n00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.480\nsingle blue star does not reset the cluster’s\n\n00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:47.960\nage.\n", "variable-distance": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520\nA pulsating star changes its own structure and\n\n00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.460\nbrightness. Watch its radius and light curve vary\n\n00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:09.180\ntogether. Not every variable works this way: an\n\n00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.540\neclipsing binary dims because another star blocks light.\n\n00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.840\nThe pulsation shown here is schematic.\n\n00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.900\nMeasure from one brightness maximum to the next\n\n00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:21.060\nequivalent maximum. That interval is the period. The\n\n00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.740\nheight of the variation is its amplitude, a\n\n00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:27.260\ndifferent quantity. Here, the luminous pulsator represents the\n\n00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.260\nclass used in the next distance calibration step.\n\n00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.040\nIts period is measured in days.\n\n00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.900\nIdentify the variable’s type and apply its calibrated\n\n00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.860\nperiod luminosity relation. Within a given Cepheid calibration,\n\n00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:43.780\nlonger periods indicate greater luminosity. Then compare intrinsic\n\n00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:47.760\nluminosity with received brightness, correcting for extinction. For\n\n00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.620\nequal luminosity, four times fainter means twice as\n\n00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:51.220\nfar away.\n", "agb-recycling": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.660\nAfter core helium runs out, a Sun-like star\n\n00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:05.100\nhas a carbon and oxygen core surrounded by\n\n00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.820\nburning shells. Helium burns closer to the core,\n\n00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.500\nwith hydrogen burning farther out. The huge, cool\n\n00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.940\nenvelope makes this an asymptotic giant branch star.\n\n00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.920\nA cool surface does not mean a cool\n\n00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:16.400\ninterior.\n\n00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:20.980\nHydrogen burning supplies fresh helium. Helium shell burning\n\n00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:24.480\ncan become unstable, producing thermal pulses. The shells\n\n00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.280\nchange their activity while the envelope expands and\n\n00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.220\nreadjusts. This is different from the earlier helium\n\n00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.480\nflash that began helium burning in the core.\n\n00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.620\nThe location of the burning matters as much\n\n00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:35.260\nas the fuel.\n\n00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:39.820\nHelium fusion makes carbon. Deep convection can carry\n\n00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:43.000\nprocessed material toward the surface, and strong winds\n\n00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.020\ncarry it into space. Follow that chain: production,\n\n00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.260\nmixing, then loss. The lecture emphasizes these giant\n\n00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.680\nstars as a major source of the galaxy’s\n\n00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.540\ncarbon. Their discarded material can become part of\n\n00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:55.960\nlater stars and planets.\n", "planetary-nebula": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.680\nAs a Sun-like giant loses its envelope, a\n\n00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:05.720\nsmall, hot core is exposed. The expanding gas\n\n00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.140\nand the central remnant are different objects. The\n\n00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.400\nremnant becomes a white dwarf, roughly Earth-sized, which\n\n00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.820\ngradually cools and fades. A planetary nebula is\n\n00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.680\nthe discarded gas, not a planet-forming disk.\n\n00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.540\nUltraviolet radiation from the hot central star ionizes\n\n00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.600\nthe surrounding gas. That gas produces bright emission\n\n00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.140\nlines. But a telescope pointed at the central\n\n00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:29.820\nstar also collects its stellar spectrum. So the\n\n00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.520\nspectrum depends on which region you observe. Move\n\n00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.300\nthe observing window from the gas to the\n\n00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.580\nstar and watch the contribution change.\n\n00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.190\nPlanetary nebulae spread out and fade over tens\n\n00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:43.270\nof thousands of years. Rings, lobes, and viewing\n\n00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:45.550\nangle can make their shapes look very different.\n\n00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:48.310\nCompanion stars can help shape the outflow; a\n\n00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:50.710\nparticular shape alone does not prove a companion.\n\n00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:53.750\nFor this course, follow the stated assumption that\n\n00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:55.910\nthe Sun forms a planetary nebula.\n", "massive-winds": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120\nThe lecture calls stars above about eight solar\n\n00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:07.040\nmasses massive. They are uncommon compared with lower-mass\n\n00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.500\nstars. Greater core pressure and temperature allow faster\n\n00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.620\nnuclear reactions. Although a massive star starts with\n\n00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.640\nmore fuel, its much greater luminosity uses that\n\n00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.880\nfuel faster, giving it a shorter life.\n\n00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:22.210\nStrong radiation can drive material away from a\n\n00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:25.350\nmassive star. Over its lifetime, winds and outbursts\n\n00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.890\nleave gas around it. When the star eventually\n\n00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:30.570\nexplodes, the explosion can run into that previously\n\n00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:34.090\nexpelled material. This explains the lecture’s dirty environment:\n\n00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:36.070\nthe surroundings carry a history of the star’s\n\n00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:37.350\nown mass loss.\n\n00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:42.410\nIf enough hydrogen-rich envelope is removed, hotter, helium-rich\n\n00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:45.050\nlayers become exposed. This can produce a Wolf\n\n00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:47.910\nRayet star. The star has changed what we\n\n00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.110\ncan see at its surface; the lost material\n\n00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:53.410\nremains outside it. Do not identify every glowing\n\n00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:56.310\nshell as a planetary nebula. The progenitor and\n\n00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:57.590\nthe physical process matter.\n", "onion-burning": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.620\nWhen a massive star exhausts one core fuel,\n\n00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:05.140\nthe core contracts and heats. If it gets\n\n00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.400\nhot enough, a heavier fuel ignites. Earlier fuels\n\n00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.480\ncan keep burning in surrounding shells. Repeated cycles\n\n00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.580\nbuild a layered interior, and the later burning\n\n00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.520\nstages become much shorter. The whole star does\n\n00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.280\nnot burn one element everywhere.\n\n00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.520\nFor the five elements in the class ordering\n\n00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.680\nexercise, go inward through hydrogen, helium, carbon, silicon,\n\n00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.340\nand iron. This selected list leaves out other\n\n00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:31.040\nlayers in a detailed model. The center contains\n\n00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.740\nthe most processed material. Keep direction straight: reversing\n\n00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.860\nthe question to center outward reverses the order.\n\n00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:42.000\nFusion toward the iron group releases energy, but\n\n00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.420\nfurther fusion does not provide the same energy\n\n00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:47.240\nsource. Iron therefore marks the end of the\n\n00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:50.460\nnormal core fusion sequence. Silicon can still be\n\n00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.820\nthe heaviest correct answer when iron is absent\n\n00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.780\nfrom the choices. Elements beyond iron require other\n\n00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:58.880\npathways; supernovae are not their only source.\n", "core-collapse": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.220\nAn iron core can grow toward the roughly\n\n00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:06.780\none-point-four solar-mass limit for electron degeneracy support. That\n\n00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.060\nis the core’s mass, not the original mass\n\n00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.700\nof the whole star. The core cannot restore\n\n00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.440\nsupport by ordinary iron fusion. Contraction raises its\n\n00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.320\ntemperature and density, setting up reactions that make\n\n00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.520\nsupport fail faster.\n\n00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.940\nElectrons combine with protons to form neutrons and\n\n00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.720\nneutrinos. Fewer electrons remain to provide degeneracy pressure.\n\n00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.440\nAt the same time, energetic photons break apart\n\n00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:34.420\nheavy nuclei and consume energy. Support weakens, contraction\n\n00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.360\nspeeds up, and an Earth-sized core collapses toward\n\n00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.840\na neutron-star scale in a fraction of a\n\n00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:40.220\nsecond.\n\n00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.440\nAt nuclear density, the stiffening matter can halt\n\n00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.060\nthe inner collapse and launch a shock. The\n\n00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.800\nshock may stall; neutrino heating can help revive\n\n00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.520\nit. If the explosion succeeds, the envelope is\n\n00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.520\nexpelled and a compact remnant remains. A neutron\n\n00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.260\nstar has a mass limit too, so continued\n\n00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.580\ncollapse can instead produce a black hole.\n", "supernova-energy": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880\nIn the lecture’s simplified collapse budget, about ninety\n\n00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.480\nnine percent of the energy escapes in neutrinos.\n\n00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:08.520\nAbout one percent remains for other channels. That\n\n00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.760\nremainder is not the same as visible light.\n\n00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:13.880\nFollow the escaping particles separately from the expanding,\n\n00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.000\nenergized gas.\n\n00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:19.230\nThe first hot fireball follows shock heating. Later,\n\n00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:22.450\nradioactive nuclei deposit more energy as they decay.\n\n00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.790\nNickel fifty six changes into cobalt fifty six,\n\n00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:29.290\nthen iron fifty six. This decay chain can\n\n00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:31.810\nsustain the fading light; calling this process fission\n\n00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:33.930\nconfuses two different nuclear mechanisms.\n\n00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:38.590\nThis moving reconstruction illustrates selected physical relationships. A\n\n00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:41.610\nnumerical simulation instead evolves a stated model from\n\n00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.150\nequations and initial conditions. Neither realistic artwork nor\n\n00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.470\na powerful computer guarantees the right explanation. Compare\n\n00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:51.210\nthe computed predictions with observations, and keep the\n\n00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.570\nmodel’s assumptions visible.\n", "supernova-remnants": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420\nFast ejecta catch slower material around the star.\n\n00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:06.380\nAt the shock, some organized bulk motion becomes\n\n00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.360\nrandom thermal motion. The heated plasma can emit\n\n00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.120\nX rays, while other regions produce optical emission\n\n00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.640\nlines. The extended remnant is gas on a\n\n00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.320\nmuch larger scale than its central compact star.\n\n00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.360\nA Type Two supernova spectrum contains hydrogen lines.\n\n00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.600\nType One lacks those lines. That spectral distinction\n\n00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.980\ndoes not uniquely identify the explosion mechanism. A\n\n00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.760\nmassive star can lose its hydrogen envelope before\n\n00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:33.560\ncollapse, producing a hydrogen poor event in the\n\n00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.500\nType One b or One c grouping.\n\n00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:39.090\nFor the same luminosity, moving ten times farther\n\n00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:42.370\naway reduces received flux to a hundredth. That\n\n00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:45.610\ncomparison alone does not calculate biological damage. In\n\n00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:49.710\nthe lecture’s atmospheric pathway, energetic radiation reduces ozone,\n\n00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:52.690\nallowing more solar ultraviolet to reach the surface.\n\n00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:56.490\nExample distances are not sharp universal danger boundaries.\n", "sn1987a": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.660\nSupernova nineteen eighty seven A occurred in the\n\n00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.900\nLarge Magellanic Cloud. Its short neutrino burst directly\n\n00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.720\nsupported the collapse energy channel. Only a tiny\n\n00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.780\nsample was detected: most particles missed Earth, and\n\n00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.620\nmost crossing a detector passed through without interacting.\n\n00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.400\nDetected counts do not equal the total emitted.\n\n00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:21.870\nThe circumstellar ring was already present before the\n\n00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:25.390\nexplosion. Ejecta and the outward shock traveled toward\n\n00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:28.350\nit. When the shock encountered dense knots, energy\n\n00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:31.170\ndeposited in the gas caused brightening. The changing\n\n00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:34.010\nring therefore records an interaction with the progenitor’s\n\n00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:37.370\nearlier surroundings, rather than newly formed ring material.\n\n00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:40.610\nA Sun like star sheds a planetary nebula\n\n00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:44.010\nand leaves a white dwarf. Successful massive core\n\n00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.990\ncollapse can leave a neutron star; continued collapse\n\n00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:49.410\ncan lead to a black hole. Specify the\n\n00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:52.510\npathway before choosing a remnant. A missing central\n\n00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:55.570\ndetection, by itself, does not uniquely establish a\n\n00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:56.350\nblack hole.\n", "white-dwarfs": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.740\nA white dwarf is a small, hot carbon\n\n00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:05.460\nand oxygen remnant that cools after ordinary core\n\n00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.940\nfusion ends. Electron degeneracy supplies pressure: crowding forces\n\n00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:13.460\nelectrons into higher momentum states because identical electrons\n\n00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.520\ncannot all share the same quantum state. This\n\n00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.780\nis not a claim that matter becomes literally\n\n00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:19.900\nincompressible.\n\n00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.020\nThe limiting white dwarf mass is about one\n\n00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:25.600\npoint four times the Sun’s mass. This limit\n\n00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.020\napplies to a remnant supported by electron degeneracy.\n\n00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.260\nIt does not describe the original star’s birth\n\n00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:34.560\nmass. A progenitor can lose substantial material before\n\n00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.220\nleaving its core. Neutron stars have a separate\n\n00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:39.460\nsupport problem with a different mass limit.\n\n00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.910\nOn a conventional H R diagram, white dwarfs\n\n00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:45.990\nlie in the hot, low luminosity region. A\n\n00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.410\nsmall surface area can outweigh a high surface\n\n00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:51.830\ntemperature. At equal temperature, reducing radius to one\n\n00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:55.350\ntenth reduces luminosity to one hundredth. A hot\n\n00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:57.910\nsurface therefore does not guarantee a large total\n\n00:00:57.910 --> 00:00:58.770\nlight output.\n", "pulsar-evidence": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080\nJocelyn Bell recognized a regular radio pulse signal\n\n00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.780\nin nineteen sixty seven. The provisional Little Green\n\n00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.280\nMen nickname captured an early question, not a\n\n00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:12.120\ndemonstrated origin. Discovering similar sources supported a natural\n\n00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.140\nastrophysical population. Regularity is evidence to explain; it\n\n00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.800\ndoes not by itself prove intelligent transmission.\n\n00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:21.810\nThe Crab pulsar repeats in about thirty three\n\n00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.790\nmilliseconds. A rotating white dwarf could not remain\n\n00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:26.850\nbound at such a short period, but a\n\n00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:29.830\nmuch smaller neutron star can support rapid rotation.\n\n00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:32.750\nPeriod is time per cycle. Its reciprocal is\n\n00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:34.850\nfrequency: a period of two hundredths of a\n\n00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:36.870\nsecond means fifty cycles each second.\n\n00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:40.410\nA neutron star packs roughly stellar mass into\n\n00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:43.290\na city scale radius. At fixed mass, reducing\n\n00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.730\nradius by half makes the volume one eighth\n\n00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:48.630\nof its original value. The density therefore increases\n\n00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:51.270\nby a factor of eight. The Crab’s central\n\n00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:54.210\npulsar and surrounding remnant support a connection between\n\n00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.610\ncore collapse and a compact neutron star. This\n\n00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:00.270\nevidence does not make every explosion identical.\n", "spin-beams-magnetars": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200\nWith negligible external torque, contraction preserves angular momentum\n\n00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.520\nwhile reducing moment of inertia. An idealized object\n\n00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.860\nwith unchanged mass and shape that halves its\n\n00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:12.960\nradius spins four times faster. Its period becomes\n\n00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.040\none quarter as long. The familiar contracting skater\n\n00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:18.540\nanalogy explains the trend; it does not make\n\n00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.180\nthe actual Sun a neutron star.\n\n00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:23.100\nThe magnetic axis can tilt away from the\n\n00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.920\nrotation axis. As the neutron star rotates, its\n\n00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.660\ndirected emission sweeps around like a lighthouse beam.\n\n00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.360\nAn observer receives pulses when that beam crosses\n\n00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.300\nthe line of sight. If the beam misses\n\n00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.360\nEarth throughout the rotation, the star can keep\n\n00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.660\nspinning without visible beam pulses here.\n\n00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:40.650\nA magnetar is a neutron star with an\n\n00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:44.530\nexceptionally strong magnetic field and related activity. Observed\n\n00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.990\nmagnetars need not spin in milliseconds; some have\n\n00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:51.150\nsecond long periods. A rapidly rotating newborn magnetar\n\n00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.090\ncan be modeled as an energy source for\n\n00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:56.330\nsome luminous supernovae. Keep that conditional birth model\n\n00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:58.510\nseparate from every magnetar’s present rotation.\n", "lensing-evidence": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.860\nA light ray can bend past a massive\n\n00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:05.200\nobject and still escape. General relativity connects matter\n\n00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.380\nand energy with curved spacetime. The curved path\n\n00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:10.260\nchanges the direction from which we see a\n\n00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.620\nbackground source; it does not require the source\n\n00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.160\nitself to move. The rubber sheet is an\n\n00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.880\nanalogy, not a literal fabric pulled downward.\n\n00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:21.750\nWatch light from one background galaxy take different\n\n00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.490\npaths around a foreground lens. An observer can\n\n00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:28.070\nreceive several apparent images, including distorted arcs. The\n\n00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:31.070\nlens has redirected light, not created extra galaxies.\n\n00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:34.350\nA classroom photograph alone may not reveal the\n\n00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.870\nexact number of distinct background sources.\n\n00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.150\nNear a black hole, plasma emits light while\n\n00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:42.970\ngravity redirects or captures it. A bright ring\n\n00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:44.950\nsurrounds a shadow; it is not a solid\n\n00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.870\nsurface shining through the horizon. A merger also\n\n00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:51.230\nchanges spacetime and can send gravitational waves outward.\n\n00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:53.730\nThose waves are a different messenger from radio,\n\n00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:55.570\noptical light or gamma rays.\n", "burst-engines": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.820\nThe prompt gamma ray flash can end quickly,\n\n00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:06.280\nbut an afterglow may remain. Rapid follow up\n\n00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.960\nlocates that fading counterpart and can connect it\n\n00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.280\nto a host galaxy. A secure redshift provides\n\n00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.720\ndistance evidence. A nearly uniform distribution of burst\n\n00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.460\ndirections, by itself, cannot tell us how far\n\n00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:19.860\naway the bursts are.\n\n00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:22.690\nIn the lecture model for many long bursts,\n\n00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.670\na rapidly rotating massive star collapses and feeds\n\n00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:28.750\na central engine through a disk. Jets emerge\n\n00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:31.610\nthrough the surrounding star. Many short bursts instead\n\n00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:34.730\ncome from compact mergers. A neutron star merger\n\n00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:36.970\nwith both gravitational waves and a gamma ray\n\n00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:40.590\nsignal supports that second pathway. Duration alone is\n\n00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.190\nnot a unique engine diagnosis.\n\n00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.040\nNow keep the source and its distance fixed,\n\n00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.580\nbut change the jet direction. A viewer inside\n\n00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.020\nthe beam can see a bright prompt burst.\n\n00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.240\nAn off axis viewer can miss it even\n\n00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.960\nthough the explosion still occurs. Beaming makes the\n\n00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.860\ndetected sample smaller than the full event population;\n\n00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.040\nit does not turn the engine off.\n", "binary-transfer": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440\nA star can expand until it fills its\n\n00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:05.040\ncritical lobe. Gas then flows through the inner\n\n00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.080\npoint region toward the companion. The donor loses\n\n00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.100\nthis material; the receiving star gains it. These\n\n00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.060\nboundaries describe a rotating binary, so the effective\n\n00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.820\nforce balance includes rotation as well as both\n\n00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:17.680\nstars’ gravity.\n\n00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:21.650\nThe transferred gas already carries orbital angular momentum.\n\n00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.970\nIn this disk fed system it curves around\n\n00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.890\nthe compact star, strikes the outer disk and\n\n00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:29.550\ngradually moves inward. The impact makes a bright\n\n00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:33.090\nspot away from the stellar center. Gravitational energy\n\n00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:36.150\nreleased by accretion can power light without restarting\n\n00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.550\nordinary core fusion.\n\n00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.980\nThe initially more massive star normally evolves first.\n\n00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.520\nIts lower mass companion can still be on\n\n00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.080\nthe main sequence when the primary dies. If\n\n00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.540\nthe binary stays bound, that companion may become\n\n00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:52.060\na giant much later and feed the compact\n\n00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:55.060\nremnant. The primary’s death and the later donor\n\n00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.040\nexpansion are separate events.\n", "surface-bursts": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.140\nHydrogen rich gas accumulates on a neutron star.\n\n00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:06.680\nIn this lecture’s thermonuclear burst model, hydrogen burning\n\n00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.540\nbuilds a helium layer. When helium ignites rapidly,\n\n00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.320\nthe surface flares and then settles. The neutron\n\n00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.500\nstar survives and can accumulate more fuel. A\n\n00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.120\nrotating pulsar beam is a different explanation for\n\n00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.580\nregularly repeating light.\n\n00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:23.270\nA white dwarf can also receive gas, but\n\n00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:26.490\nthe classical nova ignites hydrogen in its accreted\n\n00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:30.110\nsurface layer. Expanding material makes the system brighten.\n\n00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:33.470\nThe underlying white dwarf survives, so later accretion\n\n00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:36.010\ncan replenish the fuel. This differs from an\n\n00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:38.410\nexplosion that disrupts the whole white dwarf.\n\n00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:42.220\nCompare the accretor, the explosive fuel and the\n\n00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:44.880\naftermath. Helium on a neutron star gives the\n\n00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.340\nburst model; hydrogen on a white dwarf gives\n\n00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.700\na classical nova. The full Type One A\n\n00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.880\nmodel disrupts a carbon and oxygen white dwarf.\n\n00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.820\nNo compact stellar remnant still allows expanding ejecta\n\n00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.620\nand an extended remnant of gas.\n", "thermonuclear-distance": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880\nIn the near Chandrasekhar model, an accreting carbon\n\n00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.440\nand oxygen white dwarf contracts and heats until\n\n00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.340\ncarbon burning runs away. Degeneracy pressure responds weakly\n\n00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:12.480\nto temperature, so expansion cooling initially fails to\n\n00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.940\nstabilize the burning. This is a thermonuclear disruption,\n\n00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.620\nrather than an iron core collapsing into a\n\n00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.680\nneutron star. Other Type One A progenitor channels\n\n00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:22.500\nalso exist.\n\n00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:27.450\nThe explosion makes radioactive nickel fifty six. It\n\n00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:30.870\ndecays into cobalt fifty six, then stable iron\n\n00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:34.290\nfifty six. Deposited energy from that chain heats\n\n00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.770\nthe expanding debris and sustains the light. The\n\n00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:39.310\nlater radioactive heating differs from the rapid burning\n\n00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:41.710\nthat made the unstable nuclei in the first\n\n00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:42.150\nplace.\n\n00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.080\nCalibrate the event’s luminosity before comparing it with\n\n00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.060\nreceived brightness. For equal calibrated luminosities and negligible\n\n00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.800\nextinction, an event sixteen times fainter is four\n\n00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.560\ntimes farther away. Dust can also make it\n\n00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:00.160\nfainter; ignoring that dimming overestimates distance. Standardizable candles\n\n00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.060\nneed calibration, not an assumption that every explosion\n\n00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.220\nis perfectly identical.\n", "stellar-mergers": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060\nUnstable transfer can engulf two stellar cores in\n\n00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.760\none gaseous envelope. Drag transfers orbital energy into\n\n00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.680\nthat gas while the cores spiral closer. If\n\n00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.320\nthe envelope escapes soon enough, a close binary\n\n00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.020\ncan survive. If it remains, the continuing inspiral\n\n00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.660\ncan merge the cores. The shared envelope does\n\n00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.240\nnot guarantee one outcome.\n\n00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.180\nA merger can release orbital energy and produce\n\n00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.960\na luminous red nova. In the observed example,\n\n00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.380\nan eclipsing binary’s period shortened before the outburst.\n\n00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.460\nThat changing precursor supports the merger interpretation. Despite\n\n00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.680\nthe similar name, a luminous red nova is\n\n00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:39.180\ndifferent from hydrogen igniting on a white dwarf\n\n00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:40.660\nin a classical nova.\n\n00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:43.610\nMass gain or a merger can give a\n\n00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.290\nstar more mass and mix fresh hydrogen toward\n\n00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.670\nits core. In an old cluster, the result\n\n00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.690\nmay lie above and to the blue side\n\n00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:53.490\nof the ordinary turnoff: a blue straggler. Its\n\n00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.610\nevolutionary state looks younger, but elapsed time has\n\n00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:59.370\nnot reversed. One such star does not reset\n\n00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:00.630\nthe cluster’s age.\n", "pair-instability": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.360\nSuperluminous supernovae reach unusually high intrinsic luminosities. Their\n\n00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.160\nlight curves and spectra vary, so brightness alone\n\n00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.980\ndoes not identify one engine. On the absolute\n\n00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.920\nmagnitude scale, more negative means brighter in the\n\n00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.280\nsame band. Apparent brightness also depends on distance\n\n00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.900\nand cannot establish this intrinsic category by itself.\n\n00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:24.590\nIn a hot, massive core, energetic photons can\n\n00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:28.270\ncreate electron and positron pairs. That change softens\n\n00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:31.590\npressure support. Gravity drives contraction, which heats the\n\n00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:34.190\ncore and accelerates advanced burning. In the full\n\n00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:37.510\ndisruption regime, explosive burning can unbind the star.\n\n00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.690\nFollow the order: support weakens, contraction heats the\n\n00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:43.130\ncore, then thermonuclear burning responds.\n\n00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.900\nAn initial stellar mass is not the same\n\n00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.460\nas a final carbon and oxygen core mass.\n\n00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.840\nMaterial outside the core, mass loss and composition\n\n00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.100\naffect the evolution. The lecture’s rough mass scales\n\n00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.080\nare model guideposts, not a guaranteed fate for\n\n00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.520\nevery heavier star. Extreme luminosity alone does not\n\n00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.840\nprove a measured core mass or a pair\n\n00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.220\ninstability explosion.\n"};
 const localCaptionUrls={};
 function captionSrc(f){if(location.protocol!=='file:'||!localCaptionText[f.id])return f.captions;return localCaptionUrls[f.id]||=(URL.createObjectURL(new Blob([localCaptionText[f.id]],{type:'text/vtt'})));}
 function html(moduleId){
  const f=window.ASTR1102_FILMS?.find(f=>f.moduleId===moduleId);if(!f)return '';
  const privateFilm=window.ASTRPrivateFilms?.[f.id],src=privateFilm?.src||f.src,poster=privateFilm?.poster||f.poster;
  return `<section class="card film-card" data-film="${esc(f.id)}"><p class="eyebrow">NARRATED EXPLAINER · ${privateFilm?'LECTURE-FIGURE EDITION':'ORIGINAL TEACHING ANIMATION'}</p><h2>${esc(f.title)}</h2><p class="small muted">${esc(f.source)} · ${esc(f.voice)}</p><video controls playsinline preload="metadata" poster="${esc(poster)}" aria-label="${esc(f.title)}"><source src="${esc(src)}" type="video/mp4"><track kind="captions" src="${esc(captionSrc(f))}" srclang="en" label="English" default>Your browser cannot play this film. Use the download link below.</video><div class="film-chapters" aria-label="Explainer chapters">${f.stages.map((s,i)=>`<button type="button" class="btn" data-film-seek="${s.start}" aria-label="Jump to ${esc(s.title)}">${i+1}. ${esc(s.title)}</button>`).join('')}</div><p class="small" data-film-stage role="status"></p><div class="toolbar"><label>Playback speed <select data-film-speed>${[.75,1,1.25,1.5,2].map(v=>`<option value="${v}"${v===1?' selected':''}>${v}×</option>`).join('')}</select></label><a class="btn" href="${esc(src)}" download="astronomy-${esc(f.id)}.mp4">Download film</a></div><details><summary>Transcript and lecture evidence</summary>${f.stages.map(s=>`<h3>${esc(s.title)}</h3><p>${esc(s.text)}</p>`).join('')}<p class="small muted">${esc(f.disclosure)} ${privateFilm?'The original lecture figure appears beside the model. The figure is unchanged; the model is a teaching reconstruction.':'Import the private sources-and-films pack to watch the edition with the supplied lecture image beside the model.'}</p><a class="btn" href="${esc(f.transcriptSrc)}" download>Download transcript</a>${!privateFilm&&window.ASTRSourcePack?'<button type="button" class="btn" data-import-films>Import course-image films</button>':''}</details><p class="small muted">Watching is study support; it does not award mastery.</p></section>`;
 }
 function bind(root){
  root.querySelectorAll('[data-film]').forEach(card=>{
   const f=window.ASTR1102_FILMS.find(f=>f.id===card.dataset.film),v=card.querySelector('video'),status=card.querySelector('[data-film-stage]');
   const update=()=>{const s=f.stages.filter(s=>s.start<=v.currentTime+.05).at(-1)||f.stages[0];status.textContent=s.title;card.querySelectorAll('[data-film-seek]').forEach(b=>b.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(Number(b.dataset.filmSeek)===s.start)));};
   card.querySelectorAll('[data-film-seek]').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{const seek=()=>{v.currentTime=Number(b.dataset.filmSeek);update();};if(v.readyState>=1)seek();else{v.addEventListener('loadedmetadata',seek,{once:true});v.load();}});
   card.querySelector('[data-film-speed]').onchange=e=>v.playbackRate=Number(e.target.value);
   v.ontimeupdate=update;v.onerror=()=>status.textContent='This film could not load. Reload or use the downloaded copy.';update();
   card.querySelector('[data-import-films]')?.addEventListener('click',()=>window.ASTRSourcePack.choose());
  });
 }
 window.ASTRFilms={html,bind};
})();
